body:
Writes the parity assessment into the repo so people and agents can see where PrintCraft is lacking and where it's going.

- **ROADMAP.md:**
  - refreshed numbers: 804 features, 49.5% shipped, P0 88%, effort about 30–35%;
  - new **Honest assessment** section, by dimension (features, effort, foundations, robustness, fidelity, product readiness) and by area, including what "shipped" does and doesn't mean;
  - new **Where we're lacking and where we're going**: a ranked gap list (own renderer, hardening, a fidelity harness against Acrobat, editing existing content, Pro workflows, 1.0 polish), plus the owner's decisions on updates;
  - two new risks and a log entry.
- **README.md:** the stale "What's next" (it listed shipped features as upcoming) becomes an honest status summary that links to the roadmap.
- **CLAUDE.md:** every agent session reads both sections before choosing work; "shipped" means "exists and tested", not "as good as Acrobat".

Docs only.

🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)

## What this is

On a machine whose display language has a catalog, most of the `ui-egui` integration tests fail. Mine is a Spanish Windows; a French, Russian, Chinese, Japanese, Portuguese, Czech or Telugu one does the same. It is the tests, not the product, and the intent is already written down in `i18n::system_lang`:

```rust
// Tests drive the UI by its English labels whatever the developer's locale is.
if cfg!(test) {
    return Lang::EN;
}
```

That covers the unit tests. The integration tests link the library as it ships, where `cfg!(test)` is off, so `PdfCraftApp::new()` leaves `language` on `auto`, the shell is drawn in the system language, and `get_by_label("Accessibility Checker Options")` finds nothing. `tests/theme.rs` and `tests/create.rs` already work around it with `app.set_option("language", "en")`; the other harnesses don't, and CI never sees it because its locale is English.

## What this does

Pins the language the same way in every harness, 119 of them across 39 files, all the same line right after the app is built. The comment in `system_lang` now says why the harnesses have to do it, so the next one copied from an existing test keeps the line rather than quietly depending on the developer's locale.

The tests go on reading the shell by its English labels, which is the right thing for them to do — `tests/control.rs` is the one that checks labels change with the language, and it asks the catalog for them.

Also in here, the same class of problem one layer down: `i18n::tests::windows_display_language_query_returns_a_locale_tag` treats the return of `windows_ui_language()` as a single tag. It is the preferred-language list, one tag per line, which is what the doc comment says and what the only caller needs, since `first_supported` splits on `'\n'`. On any Windows with two or more display languages the assertion trips over the newline, `\n` being neither empty nor ASCII alphanumeric. It now asserts the shape of every line, and that there is at lea…

