## 1. The corners start on the document's edges

`camera_edges::detect_document` finds a photographed document's corners, pure Rust and deterministic, no models or dependencies:

1. shrink the picture to 400 px on its long side, blur, Sobel gradients thinned (non-maximum suppression) and thresholded against the 98th percentile;
2. a Hough transform in which each edge votes only within 6° of its own normal (a jagged, aliased edge's local gradient leans towards the axes, so ±2° missed slightly tilted sheets);
3. pairs of near-horizontal and near-vertical lines make convex quadrilaterals covering at least 12% of the picture; one is kept only when every side lies on a matching edge along at least 55% of its length, and the best is the one with the largest area × (mean side support)², so a slightly larger quad with one side off the sheet loses to the sheet;
4. each side is refitted through its edge points (total least squares) and the corners are intersected again;
5. `None` when nothing convinces; the UI then falls back to the 5% inset.

`Session::detect_camera_corners` returns them in user space. `page_enhance_camera` finds the corners itself when `corners` is omitted (and reports the corners it used and `detected: true`, or an error asking for `corners`). The command starts the handles on them.

Tests, all on synthetic photos (corners within 6 px on 900–1200 px pictures): a plain table, text on the sheet (text lines must not shrink the quad), wood grain with a shadow, strong perspective (a card from an angle), a 30° turn, a sheet running off the picture (nothing invented), no document and white on white (`None`), bad input; end to end through the engine and the tool. I also tried it locally on a real phone photo of a light card on a patterned fabric: the card was found and straightened (that photo isn't in the repository).

